---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Note from "../../components/Note.astro";
const title = "second brain — the dysfunctional executives";
---
<BaseLayout {title}>
  <article class="container">
    <h1>second brain</h1>
    <p>a second brain is an external system for <span class="hl">capturing, organizing, and retrieving</span> your ideas. instead of keeping everything in your head, you offload it into a trusted structure so your mind is free to think, not store.</p>

    <p>the concept was popularized by tiago forte. the core idea: <span class="hl-pink ul-solid">your brain is for having ideas, not holding them.</span></p>

    <h2>the capture habit</h2>
    <p>everything starts with capture. if an idea crosses your mind and you don't write it down, it's gone. the capture habit means you always have a way to record a thought within seconds.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>keep a capture tool on every device you use</li>
      <li>the tool matters less than the habit: notes app, voice memo, scrap of paper</li>
      <li><span class="hl">capture first, organize later.</span> don't sort while you're thinking</li>
      <li><span class="ul-wavy">if it takes more than 10 seconds to capture, you'll stop doing it</span></li>
    </ul>

    <h2>organize by actionability, not by topic</h2>
    <p>traditional folders sort by subject (work, personal, hobbies). a second brain sorts by <span class="hl-blue">what you can do with the information right now</span>.</p>

    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>folder</th>
          <th>what goes here</th>
          <th>when to review</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><span class="hl-mint">projects</span></td>
          <td>active goals with deadlines</td>
          <td>weekly</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><span class="hl-blue">areas</span></td>
          <td>ongoing responsibilities (health, finances, career)</td>
          <td>monthly</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><span class="hl-oat">resources</span></td>
          <td>topics of interest, reference material</td>
          <td>as needed</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>archive</td>
          <td>completed or inactive items</td>
          <td>never (but searchable)</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>progressive summarization</h2>
    <p>you don't need to read everything deeply on first pass. progressive summarization means you add <span class="ul-dashed">layers of emphasis over time</span>, only going deeper on notes that prove useful.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>layer 0: capture the original source (highlight, clip, screenshot)</li>
      <li>layer 1: <span class="hl">bold the key passages</span> that matter to you</li>
      <li>layer 2: highlight within the bolded sections</li>
      <li>layer 3: write an executive summary in your own words</li>
      <li>layer 4: <span class="hl-mint ul-wavy-mint">remix: combine notes into something new</span> (a post, a project, a framework)</li>
    </ul>

    <Note variant="try">start with just capture. don't worry about folders or tags. the organization emerges once you have enough notes to see patterns.</Note>

    <h2>setup checklist</h2>
    <p>work through these steps to build your own second brain. click items as you complete them.</p>

    <Note variant="bench" label="setup checklist">
      <ul>
        <li>choose one capture tool (obsidian, notion, apple notes, or even a text file)</li>
        <li>install it on your phone and computer</li>
        <li>create four folders: projects, areas, resources, archive</li>
        <li>capture 10 things you've been keeping in your head</li>
        <li>set a weekly review reminder (15 minutes)</li>
        <li>move completed project notes to archive</li>
        <li>bold the most useful sentence in 5 existing notes</li>
        <li>write a one-line summary at the top of 3 notes you revisit often</li>
        <li>delete or archive notes you haven't touched in 6 months</li>
        <li>teach someone else how your system works (this reveals gaps)</li>
      </ul>
    </Note>

    <div class="callout">pro tip: the best second brain is the one you actually use. don't over-engineer it. start messy, refine later.</div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
